//? Title: Independent Component Analysis: Beyond Decorrelation
//? Subtitle: Exact FastICA updates, source matching and limits of identification
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Several measured signals may each contain a different mixture of the same hidden
sources. Recovering those sources requires assumptions beyond making the measurements
uncorrelated. Independent component analysis uses an instantaneous linear mixing model
and non-Gaussian structure to estimate a separating transform. We first construct an
exact uncorrelated-but-dependent four-point example and calculate a FastICA update by
hand. Known waveforms, independent random sources, a Gaussian control and mixture
geometry then test what the algorithm recovers and what numerical convergence cannot
establish. Iris supplies an observed-data illustration with no known latent-source
truth. Population whitening, per-component stopping, frozen transforms and one-to-one
recovery matching remain explicit throughout. All twelve non-Gaussian random-source
fits meet the angle rule, with matched mean correlations from .98949 to .99986;
Gaussian controls show why neither a high score nor numerical stopping identifies
a unique source basis.
)
\toc

> The method

>> Specify the source model and its ambiguities

Let observations satisfy \(x=\mu+As\), with \(p\) measured coordinates, \(k\le p\)
mutually independent source variables and a full-column-rank mixing matrix \(A\).
We estimate \(s\) from the observations without knowing \(A\). This is an
instantaneous linear model; it does not represent reverberation, time delays,
nonlinear mixtures or an arbitrary collection of measured variables.

Sign, scale and ordering cannot be inferred from the mixture alone. For an invertible
diagonal \(D\) and permutation \(P\),

$$
 As=(AP^TD^{-1})(DPs).
$$
\alttext(Scaling and permuting the sources can be undone in the mixing matrix without changing any observation. Thus source sign, magnitude and order are ambiguous.)

With independent non-Gaussian sources, standard ICA identification results recover
these directions up to those ambiguities; at most one source can be Gaussian. A
multi-source Gaussian subspace permits additional orthogonal rotations with the
same distribution. Numerical contrast optimization on a finite sample does not
prove independence or select a physically meaningful interpretation.

Independence means the joint probability law factorizes. Zero covariance constrains
only one second moment. A nonlinear dependence can remain even when every pairwise
correlation is zero. The central limit theorem motivates searching for non-Gaussian
projections, but it does not order every finite weighted sum as “more Gaussian than
each constituent” under every contrast. We use specific contrast approximations,
which can have local or spurious stationary directions and sampling error.

>> Population whitening removes covariance, not all dependence

For the \(n\) complete fitting rows, let \(X_c\) be the centered row matrix. Compute
its population-divisor covariance and retain the leading \(k\) positive eigenvalues:

$$
 C=\frac1nX_c^TX_c=E\Lambda E^T,\qquad
 K=E_k\Lambda_k^{-1/2},\qquad Z=X_cK,\qquad \frac1nZ^TZ=I_k.
$$
\alttext(Population covariance divides the centered cross-product by n. The retained eigenvectors divided by square roots of their eigenvalues form a whitening map. Multiplication by that map gives identity population covariance.)

Every orthogonal rotation of \(Z\) retains that identity covariance. Thus covariance
alone cannot choose the independent orientation. PCA coordinates are not automatically
white: whitening additionally divides each retained score by its standard deviation.
PCA truncation discards information before ICA and depends on the original feature
units. Native ICA centers but does not standardize the input feature columns.

The diagram uses a two-source example with mixing matrix rows (1,.8),(.8,1).
Its thin mixture parallelogram becomes a white diamond in PCA coordinates, then a
square in the independent coordinates. Both latter clouds have identity covariance.
Rendering needs LaTeX, PGF/TikZ, standalone, Latin Modern and dvisvgm; export reads
the local SVG and does not rerun TeX.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/ica_whitening_rotation.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/ica_whitening_rotation.tikz > build/ica_whitening_rotation.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/ica_whitening_rotation.svg build/ica_whitening_rotation.dvi > build/ica_whitening_rotation.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the local whitening and rotation diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the local whitening and rotation diagram.
// \image(figures/ica_whitening_rotation.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Whitening leaves an orientation problem: a white diamond and a white square can have different dependence.)
\alttext(Three panels show mixed four-corner source values in a thin parallelogram, their PCA-whitened diamond, and the independent square. Arrows distinguish covariance whitening from the contrast-based rotation.)

>> Derive the deflationary fixed-point update

For one unit direction \(w\) in white coordinates, approximate non-Gaussianity using
a smooth contrast \(G\). Under the constraint \(w^Tw=1\), stationarity requires
\(E[z\,g(w^Tz)]-\beta w=0\), with \(g=G'\). A Newton-style approximation replaces
the expectation involving \(zz^Tg'\) by \(E[g']I\). Rescaling the resulting direction
leads to the FastICA update below. This is a fixed-point method, not a line search
that guarantees a monotone empirical contrast at every iteration.

$$
 u=\frac1n\sum_i z_i g(w^Tz_i)-\left[\frac1n\sum_i g'(w^Tz_i)\right]w,
 \quad u\leftarrow u-\sum_{j<c}(u^Tw_j)w_j,\quad
 w^+=\frac{u}{\|u\|_2},\quad e=1-|{w^+}^Tw|.
$$
\alttext(Averages of whitened rows times the contrast derivative, minus average derivative-of-derivative times the old direction, give a raw update. Project off previously extracted directions and normalize. A sign-invariant angle change measures numerical progress.)

Deflation extracts directions sequentially. Two projection passes keep new directions
orthogonal to the previous ones. The three implemented derivative pairs are

$$
 \begin{aligned}
 \text{logcosh}:&\quad g(u)=\tanh u,&g'(u)&=1-\tanh^2u,\\
 \text{exp}:&\quad g(u)=u e^{-u^2/2},&g'(u)&=(1-u^2)e^{-u^2/2},\\
 \text{cube}:&\quad g(u)=u^3,&g'(u)&=3u^2.
 \end{aligned}
$$
\alttext(Logcosh uses tanh and its derivative; exp uses u times a Gaussian-shaped exponential and its derivative; cube uses cubic and quadratic functions.)

These are contrasts for a non-Gaussianity approximation, not direct independence
measurements. Empirical excess kurtosis is \(E[y^4]/E[y^2]^2-3\) for centered sources.
A nonzero value rules out Gaussian marginal shape, but zero kurtosis does not imply
a Gaussian law or independence. Bounded tanh is less sensitive to large projections
than the cubic contrast; no contrast is universally best.

>> Fitted maps, diagnostics and numerical contracts

Let \(R\) contain the extracted orthonormal directions as rows. The fitted source,
unmixing and mixing maps are

$$
 S=ZR^T=X_cB^T,\qquad B=RK^T,\qquad
 A_{\rm fit}=E_k\Lambda_k^{1/2}R^T=B^+,\qquad
 A_{\rm fit}B=E_kE_k^T.
$$
\alttext(Source rows are whitened rows multiplied by the rotation transpose. The original-unit unmixing is rotation times whitening transpose. Its pseudo-inverse reconstructs the retained PCA subspace, with full reconstruction only when that subspace spans the observations.)

The columns of \(A_{\rm fit}\) are feature-space mixing patterns. The rows of \(B\)
are unmixing covectors; they are not interchangeable geometric arrows. With fewer
components, reconstruction is the retained PCA projection regardless of the ICA
rotation. A tiny full-rank reconstruction error therefore checks algebra, not
source separation or statistical independence.

`ICA` drops rows with null selected features jointly and exposes their original
indices through `kept_row_indices()`. Retained values must be finite, feature names
unique and numeric, the iteration budget positive, and \(0<\mathrm{tol}<1\) finite.
A common feature-scale factor stabilizes covariance formation. The relative rank
cutoff is \(\max(n,p)\epsilon\lambda_{\max}\); asking for too many nondegenerate
dimensions is an error. A zero deflated update raises an exception instead of NaNs.

`converged()` means every direction had \(e<\mathrm{tol}\) within its budget. It is
not an accuracy or independence certificate. `component_diagnostics()` retains
individual stopping flags, angle changes, population variances and kurtoses;
`convergence_history()` records every completed direction update before final signs.
Even an exhausted fit retains orthonormal directions and a valid reconstruction map.
The first training value with largest absolute magnitude fixes each component's sign
positive; components remain in extraction order. Seed reproducibility assumes the
same standard library's normal-distribution implementation, not every platform.

`transform()` always uses the fitted means and unmixing map, so an observation gives
the same answer alone or in a batch. Query null/nonfinite values are rejected rather
than omitted. `inverse_transform()` adds back the training mean. A known-source
benchmark uses `ica_source_matches()` to maximize the total absolute correlation
with a one-to-one Hungarian assignment. It also supplies sign/scale/offset alignment
for plotting. Truth is used only for scoring, never for fitting; unknown observed
data have no such recovery score.

Covariance formation costs \(O(np^2)\), with a bounded-sweep \(O(p^3)\) eigensolve.
For \(T\) total updates across \(k\) components, fixed-point work is
\(O(T(nk+k^2))\). Retained arrays and history use
\(O(n(p+k)+p^2+k^2+Tk)\) storage. Source matching adds \(O(nk^2+k^3)\) work.

> Simple examples

>> Four equally likely points: decorrelated but dependent

Take all four combinations of \((s_1,s_2)\in\{-1,1\}^2\), each with probability one
quarter. The sources are independent, centered and have variance one. Rotate them:
\(u=(s_1+s_2)/\sqrt2\), \(v=(s_1-s_2)/\sqrt2\). The rotated variables also have
variance one and covariance zero, but exactly one is zero in every row. Thus
\(P(u=0)=P(v=0)=1/2\), while \(P(u=0,v=0)=0\ne1/4\). Equivalently,
\(u^2+v^2=2\) and their squared values are perfectly negatively correlated.
The native Pearson call below supplies only its coefficient; its inference assumptions
are not applied to this discrete four-point construction.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ica_dependence.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::vector<double> s1 = {-1, -1, 1, 1}, s2 = {-1, 1, -1, 1};
  std::vector<double>       u, v, u2, v2;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
    u.push_back((s1[i] + s2[i]) / std::sqrt(2.));
    v.push_back((s1[i] - s2[i]) / std::sqrt(2.));
    u2.push_back(u.back() * u.back());
    v2.push_back(v.back() * v.back());
  }
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  left.points(s1, s2, "independent corners", {37, 99, 235}, 7)
      .title("Independent square")
      .x_label("source 1")
      .y_label("source 2")
      .x_limits(-2, 2)
      .y_limits(-2, 3);
  right.points(u, v, "zero covariance", {220, 38, 38}, 7)
      .title("White, but dependent")
      .x_label("rotated u")
      .y_label("rotated v")
      .x_limits(-2, 2)
      .y_limits(-2, 3);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/ica_dependence.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("s1", s1);
  table.add_column("s2", s2);
  table.add_column("u", u);
  table.add_column("v", v);
  table.add_column("u_squared", u2);
  table.add_column("v_squared", v2);
  std::size_t both_zero = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i)
    both_zero += u[i] == 0 && v[i] == 0;
  std::print(
      "Pearson(u,v)={:.8f}; Pearson(u squared,v squared)={:.8f}.\nJoint zeros: {}/4; marginal "
      "zeros: {}/4 and {}/4.\n\n{}\n",
      dm::stats::pearson_correlation_test(u, v).estimate1,
      dm::stats::pearson_correlation_test(u2, v2).estimate1,
      both_zero,
      std::count(u.begin(), u.end(), 0.),
      std::count(v.begin(), v.end(), 0.),
      table.to_md(8));
}
```
