// result_end
\caption(Iris illustrates fitted coordinates and numerical checks, with no known-source recovery or causal claim.)
\alttext(A scatter of the first two full-fit components is coloured by external species labels. Bars show the four marginal excess kurtoses. Tables preview the measured data, compare reduced/full reconstructions and report every component’s angle stopping and population moments.)

For Iris, both dimension choices meet the angle rule. Two retained PCA dimensions
account for .977685206 of the original covariance trace, with reconstruction RMSE
.1591888 cm per feature cell. Four dimensions reconstruct to floating-point precision.
These are retained-subspace diagnostics, not an ICA variance ranking or an estimate
of latent-source accuracy.

> Conclusion

The four-point example proves directly that whitening does not establish independence.
The exact cubic update explains how non-Gaussian shape can select a direction after
whitening, and the known-source benchmarks evaluate recovery with a one-to-one match.
The Gaussian control illustrates an identification limit that numerical convergence
cannot remove. The deterministic waveform recovery is useful evidence for that
construction, rather than a proof that all signals sharing a time index are independent.

Mixing-pattern geometry, reconstruction accuracy, contrast stopping and source recovery
answer different questions. Iris has no source truth, so its fitted coordinates and
kurtoses remain a descriptive illustration. In practice, source-model assumptions,
finite-sample stability, component count and the downstream use all need assessment;
a converged FastICA fit alone does not settle them.

> References

- P. Comon (1994), “Independent component analysis, a new concept?”, Signal Processing
  36(3), 287–314. https://doi.org/10.1016/0165-1684(94)90029-9.
- A. Hyvärinen and E. Oja (1997), “A fast fixed-point algorithm for independent
  component analysis”, Neural Computation 9(7), 1483–1492.
  https://www.cs.helsinki.fi/u/ahyvarin/papers/NC97.pdf.
- A. Hyvärinen (1999), “Fast and robust fixed-point algorithms for independent
  component analysis”, IEEE Transactions on Neural Networks 10(3), 626–634.
  https://www.cs.helsinki.fi/u/ahyvarin/papers/TNN99_reprint.pdf.
- A. Hyvärinen and E. Oja (2000), “Independent component analysis: algorithms and
  applications”, Neural Networks 13(4–5), 411–430.
  https://doi.org/10.1016/S0893-6080(00)00026-5.

